import java.util.*;

// Indexed reads / iteration -> ArrayList (cache-friendly, O(1) get)
List<String> rows = new ArrayList<>(50_000);
for (int i = 0; i < 50_000; i++) rows.add("row-" + i);
for (String row : rows) process(row);   // sequential, cache hits

// Genuine queue/stack pattern -> ArrayDeque (O(1) ends, no node overhead)
Deque<String> tasks = new ArrayDeque<>();
tasks.addLast("enqueue-me");            // O(1)
String head = tasks.pollFirst();        // O(1) dequeue
static void process(String s) {}
```

Pick LinkedList only when you truly need List + Deque on one object; otherwise ArrayList (reads) or ArrayDeque (queues) wins.
